            "When a patient meets the suspected case definition, the <strong>first hour</strong> decides the outbreak. The patient is <strong>isolated</strong> at once — a separate room or a screened space with its own entrance, a dedicated toilet or commode, and a line that nobody crosses without protection — and the number of staff in contact is reduced to the smallest trained team. The <strong>notification</strong> goes by telephone to the district health directorate and the rapid response team <em>now</em>, not at the end of the shift. <strong>No blood is taken</strong> and no invasive procedure performed until full protective equipment is on; the diagnostic sample is drawn by trained staff in full PPE, packed in triple packaging and transported only through the surveillance system to Noguchi, never sent to the hospital laboratory, where an unprotected technician would spin it. While waiting, the team starts a malaria test and antimalarial treatment and empirical antibiotics, because malaria and typhoid are commoner and may coexist, and begins oral rehydration. Every person who has touched the patient, his fluids or his bedding since the onset of illness is listed, beginning with the staff; the ambulance that brought him and the room where he waited are decontaminated with 0.5 per cent chlorine. If the test is negative the patient returns to ordinary care with gratitude for a false alarm; if it is positive he is moved by the trained team to a <strong>treatment unit</strong> — a facility with a one-way flow from a low-risk zone (changing rooms, pharmacy, staff rest) through the donning area to the high-risk zone (suspected and confirmed wards kept separate, a morgue, a waste zone) and out through doffing with its chlorine footbaths and mirrors — staffed by a team that works short shifts because the heat inside the equipment is itself a danger.",
