            "<strong>Rabies</strong> is a lyssavirus carried in the saliva of infected mammals — in Ghana almost always the domestic <strong>dog</strong>, occasionally cats, livestock bitten by dogs, and bats — and transmitted by bites, scratches and licks on broken skin or mucous membranes. From the wound the virus travels slowly along the nerves to the brain, over an incubation of one to three months (as short as a week with deep bites to the face, head and hands, and in small children, and as long as a year or more), and then causes an encephalitis that is <strong>fatal in every case</strong> once symptoms begin: fever and tingling or pain at the healed bite site, then the furious form with agitation, hallucinations, hydrophobia (painful spasms of the throat at the sight or sound of water), aerophobia, hypersalivation and convulsions, or the paralytic form of ascending flaccid paralysis mistaken for Guillain–Barré, and death within one to two weeks. There is no treatment — the patient is sedated, kept comfortable in a quiet room, nursed with gloves, gown, mask and eye protection against the saliva, and the family counselled and the contacts assessed — which is why the whole of rabies nursing happens <strong>at the bite</strong>, where it is entirely preventable.",
