        "In the first hour the patient is isolated, the district is telephoned, no blood is drawn until full PPE is on, the sample goes through the surveillance system to Noguchi in triple packaging, malaria is treated and the contact list begins with the staff; the treatment unit runs a one-way flow with full PPE donned and doffed under a trained observer, 0.5 per cent chlorine for surfaces and bodies and 0.05 per cent for gloved hands, no avoidable sharps and infectious waste handled on site.",
        "Supportive care — litres of oral and intravenous fluid with potassium and glucose, antiemetics, paracetamol and no NSAIDs, oxygen, blood products, treatment of malaria and sepsis — saves most Lassa patients and many Ebola patients; ribavirin treats early Lassa, monoclonal antibodies and the Ervebo vaccine exist for Zaire Ebola; contacts are followed for 21 days with twice-daily temperatures, the dead are buried within 24 hours by a trained team with the family present at a distance, and survivors are followed for persistent virus, deafness, eye disease and stigma.",
        "Rabies is always fatal once symptoms begin and always preventable at the bite: wash with soap and running water for fifteen minutes, categorise the exposure, give the cell-culture vaccine into the deltoid or thigh (never the buttock) on days 0, 3, 7 and 14–28 for category II and III, infiltrate rabies immunoglobulin into and around every category III wound on day 0, add tetanus and antibiotic cover, do not suture tightly, observe the dog for ten days, report the bite, and support the vaccination of seventy per cent of the dogs.",
        "Tetanus follows a contaminated wound or an unclean cord after three to twenty-one days with trismus, risus sardonicus, opisthotonus, spasms triggered by stimuli and autonomic storms; it is nursed in a dark quiet room with grouped gentle care, immunoglobulin 500 units, metronidazole, wound debridement, titrated diazepam and magnesium, nasogastric feeding, airway protection and a spasm chart, and the survivor is vaccinated because the disease gives no immunity.",
        "Tetanus prevention is three Pentavalent doses in infancy, five tetanus–diphtheria doses for women giving three, five, ten years and then lifelong protection, a booster after ten years for clean wounds and five years for dirty ones with immunoglobulin when fewer than three doses are known, clean delivery and chlorhexidine or dry cord care — the measures that eliminated maternal and neonatal tetanus in Ghana in 2011 and make every case a notifiable sentinel event.",
        "Anthrax strikes those who open or eat an animal that died suddenly — a painless black eschar with oedema, or bloody diarrhoea with high mortality — and is treated with ciprofloxacin or doxycycline and prevented by deep burial, livestock vaccination and post-exposure antibiotics, as in the Upper East outbreak of 2023; brucellosis comes from unboiled milk, leptospirosis from rat urine in flood water, and all the zoonoses are met by One Health — the nurse and the veterinary officer on the same telephone call.",
